        "answerHtml": "<!-- QUESTION 1: FEB 2020 (MAIN) Q.2 [15 MARKS] -->\n        <div class=\"q-block\">\n          <div class=\"q-header\">\n            <span class=\"q-badge\">MPMSU Feb 2020 (Main) — Q.2 [15 Marks]</span>\n            <span class=\"q-weight\">Full Long Essay | Target: 5 Handwritten Pages</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"q-text-prompt\">\n            <strong>Exam Question:</strong> a. Define Growth and Development (2) | b. Explain the principles of growth and development (5) | c. Describe the developmental milestones of an infant (0-1 year) in motor, sensory, social and language domains (8).\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"q-answer-content\">\n            <div class=\"ans-part\">\n              <div class=\"ans-part-title\">Part (a): Definitions of Growth & Development (2 Marks)</div>\n              <div class=\"ans-part-body\">\n                <p><strong>Growth:</strong> An increase in the physical size, mass, and dimension of the body or its constituent organs, resulting from cellular multiplication (hyperplasia) and cellular enlargement (hypertrophy). It is quantitative and objectively measurable in centimeters, kilograms, and percentiles.</p>\n                <p><strong>Development:</strong> A progressive, orderly maturation of physiological, neuromuscular, cognitive, and psychosocial functions and skills. It is qualitative, reflecting increased complexity of behavioral capacity and neurodevelopmental integration.</p>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"ans-part\">\n              <div class=\"ans-part-title\">Part (b): Principles of Growth and Development (5 Marks)</div>\n              <div class=\"ans-part-body\">\n                <ol>\n                  <li><strong>1. Directional Trends:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                      <li><em>Cephalocaudal (Head-to-Tail):</em> Neuromuscular control develops from the head downward (head control achieved at 3 months, sitting at 6–8 months, walking at 12–15 months).</li>\n                      <li><em>Proximodistal (Near-to-Far):</em> Development proceeds from the central axis outward toward extremities (shoulder control precedes hand grasps; whole arm swiping precedes fine pincer grasp).</li>\n                      <li><em>Differentiation:</em> Progression from simple, generalized gross responses to complex, refined, specific motor acts.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                  </li>\n                  <li><strong>2. Continuous but Non-Linear Process:</strong> Growth is continuous from conception to maturity, but proceeds at varying speeds (infancy and adolescence are periods of rapid \"growth spurts\", whereas preschool and school-age are periods of steady slow growth).</li>\n                  <li><strong>3. Individualized Rates:</strong> Every child grows at their own unique biological pace; wide normal developmental ranges exist.</li>\n                  <li><strong>4. Predictable & Sequential Order:</strong> The sequence of developmental stages is universal and invariant (e.g., sitting always precedes crawling; crawling precedes walking).</li>\n                  <li><strong>5. Interrelated Domains:</strong> Physical, motor, cognitive, social, and emotional domains mutually influence each other.</li>\n                </ol>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"ans-part\">\n              <div class=\"ans-part-title\">Part (c): Developmental Milestones of Infant (0 to 1 Year) (8 Marks)</div>\n              <div class=\"ans-part-body\">\n                <table class=\"clinical-table\">\n                  <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Age</th><th>Gross Motor Domain</th><th>Fine Motor Domain</th><th>Language & Social Domains</th></tr>\n                  </thead>\n                  <tbody>\n                    <tr>\n                      <td><strong>3 Months</strong></td>\n                      <td><strong>Complete Head Holding / Control</strong> when pulled to sit; lifts head and chest 90° in prone position.</td>\n                      <td>Hands open loosely; holds rattle momentarily when placed in hand.</td>\n                      <td>Coos, laughs aloud, chuckles; <strong>Social Smile</strong> appears in response to mother's voice.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                      <td><strong>6 Months</strong></td>\n                      <td><strong>Rolls over from prone to supine</strong> and supine to prone; sits with pelvic support (tripod fashion).</td>\n                      <td>Palmar grasp of objects; transfers objects from hand to hand; reaches for dangling toy.</td>\n                      <td>Monosyllabic babbling (\"ba-ba\", \"da-da\"); smiles at own mirror image; recognizes mother.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                      <td><strong>9 Months</strong></td>\n                      <td><strong>Sits steadily without any support</strong>; pulls self up to a standing position holding furniture.</td>\n                      <td>Immature / Crude Pincer Grasp (between thumb and index finger pad); drops objects voluntarily.</td>\n                      <td>Bisyllabic babbling; waves \"bye-bye\"; plays \"peek-a-boo\"; shows <strong>Stranger Anxiety</strong>.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                      <td><strong>12 Months (1 Year)</strong></td>\n                      <td><strong>Walks with one hand held</strong> or takes first independent steps; stands alone briefly.</td>\n                      <td><strong>Mature / Fine Pincer Grasp</strong> (picks up small raisin/pellet neatly between tips of thumb and index finger).</td>\n                      <td>Says 1–2 words with meaning (\"Mama\", \"Dada\"); understands simple verbal commands (\"Come here\").</td>\n                    </tr>\n                  </tbody>\n                </table>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------ -->\n    <!-- TOPIC 4: NATIONAL IMMUNIZATION SCHEDULE & COLD CHAIN (3/4)  -->\n    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------ -->"
