        "answerHtml": "<!-- QUESTION 1: JULY 2022 (MAIN) Q.3 [10 MARKS] -->\n        <div class=\"q-block\">\n          <div class=\"q-header\">\n            <span class=\"q-badge\">MPMSU July 2022 (Main & Supp) — Q.3 [10 Marks]</span>\n            <span class=\"q-weight\">Essay Question | Target: 4 Handwritten Pages</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"q-text-prompt\">\n            <strong>Exam Question:</strong> a. Define Population, Sample, Sampling frame and Sampling error (3) | b. Differentiate between Probability and Non-Probability sampling (3) | c. Explain various types of probability sampling techniques with nursing examples (4).\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"q-answer-content\">\n            <div class=\"ans-part\">\n              <div class=\"ans-part-title\">Part (a): Definitions of Fundamental Sampling Concepts (3 Marks)</div>\n              <div class=\"ans-part-body\">\n                <ul>\n                  <li><strong>Population (Universe):</strong> The entire aggregate or totality of individuals, cases, or objects that conform to a designated set of inclusion specifications (e.g., all registered nurses working in government hospitals in Madhya Pradesh).</li>\n                  <li><strong>Sample:</strong> A selected subset or portion of the target population chosen to participate in a research study to represent the whole.</li>\n                  <li><strong>Sampling Frame:</strong> An actual, comprehensive, objective list or directory of all eligible sampling units/elements in the accessible population from which the sample is drawn (e.g., the official staff register of a hospital).</li>\n                  <li><strong>Sampling Error:</strong> The inevitable, random statistical discrepancy between a sample statistic (e.g., sample mean $\\bar{X}$) and the true population parameter (population mean $\\mu$), caused by random fluctuation inherent in selecting a subset.</li>\n                </ul>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"ans-part\">\n              <div class=\"ans-part-title\">Part (b): Differentiating Probability vs. Non-Probability Sampling (3 Marks)</div>\n              <div class=\"ans-part-body\">\n                <table class=\"clinical-table\">\n                  <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Probability Sampling</th><th>Non-Probability Sampling</th></tr>\n                  </thead>\n                  <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Random Selection</strong></td><td>Uses strict random selection mechanisms; every element has a known, non-zero probability of being selected.</td><td>Does not involve random selection; elements chosen based on convenience, judgment, or availability.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Representativeness & Bias</strong></td><td>High representativeness; minimizes researcher selection bias.</td><td>Lower representativeness; high risk of selection bias.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Generalizability</strong></td><td>Findings can be confidently generalized to the broader target population with calculated sampling error.</td><td>Generalizability is severely limited; findings apply strictly to the immediate sample.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Use Case</strong></td><td>Preferred in quantitative, experimental, and epidemiological surveys.</td><td>Common in qualitative research, preliminary exploratory studies, or when sampling frames are unavailable.</td></tr>\n                  </tbody>\n                </table>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"ans-part\">\n              <div class=\"ans-part-title\">Part (c): Types of Probability Sampling Techniques with Nursing Examples (4 Marks)</div>\n              <div class=\"ans-part-body\">\n                <ol>\n                  <li><strong>1. Simple Random Sampling (SRS):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                      <li><em>Mechanism:</em> Every element in the sampling frame has an equal and independent chance of selection. Accomplished via lottery method or computer-generated random number table.</li>\n                      <li><em>Nursing Example:</em> Selecting 50 nurses randomly from a hospital staff list of 500 using random number software.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                  </li>\n                  <li><strong>2. Stratified Random Sampling:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                      <li><em>Mechanism:</em> The heterogeneous population is divided into mutually exclusive, homogeneous subgroups (strata) based on an important variable (e.g., clinical department, experience), and random samples are drawn proportionally from each stratum.</li>\n                      <li><em>Nursing Example:</em> In a hospital study on needle-stick injuries, dividing nurses into ICU, Ward, and OPD strata, then randomly selecting 20% of nurses from each department to ensure proportional representation.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                  </li>\n                  <li><strong>3. Systematic Random Sampling:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                      <li><em>Mechanism:</em> Selecting every $k$-th element from an ordered sampling frame, where the sampling interval $k = N / n$ (Population size / Sample size), with a random starting point between 1 and $k$.</li>\n                      <li><em>Nursing Example:</em> If $N = 1,000$ patient admission files and $n = 100$, $k = 10$. Randomly pick a number between 1 and 10 (e.g., 4). Then select files 4, 14, 24, 34, 44... up to 100 files.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                  </li>\n                  <li><strong>4. Cluster Sampling (Multi-Stage Sampling):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                      <li><em>Mechanism:</em> Used when a comprehensive sampling frame of individuals is impossible across large geographic areas. Large natural clusters (villages, districts, hospitals) are randomly selected first, then all individuals within chosen clusters are sampled.</li>\n                      <li><em>Nursing Example:</em> In a statewide study on rural childhood malnutrition in MP, randomly selecting 10 Primary Health Centers (PHCs) out of 100, then screening all infants attending those 10 PHCs.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                  </li>\n                </ol>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------ -->\n    <!-- TOPIC 5: MEASURES OF CENTRAL TENDENCY & NUMERICALS (4/4)    -->\n    <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------ -->"
